# Accessibility Rules

## Semantic HTML first
- `<button>` for actions, `<a href>` for navigation, `<dialog>` for modals.
- Never an interactive `<div>` or `<span>`.
- Outline pages with `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<header>`, `<footer>`, `<section>`.

## Keyboard
- Every interactive element reachable by Tab in document order.
- Visible focus styles. Never `outline: none` without a replacement.
- Match widget role: arrow keys in menus/tabs/listboxes, Esc closes dialogs.
- Skip-link at top of layout.

## Focus management
- Modals trap focus; restore to trigger on close.
- Move focus to `<main>` or new heading after route changes.
- Live regions: `aria-live="polite"` for non-urgent (chat, score), `"assertive"` only for critical (errors, payment).
- Don't move focus on real-time data updates.

## Forms
- Every input has `<label htmlFor>`. Placeholder is not a label.
- Group related inputs with `<fieldset>` + `<legend>`.
- Errors via `aria-describedby` + `role="alert"`.

## Images & media
- Meaningful `alt`; decorative images use `alt=""`.
- Icon-only buttons: `aria-label`.
- Captions for video; visible mute control on auto-play.

## Colour & motion
- Text contrast ≥4.5:1 (3:1 for large/bold text).
- Never colour alone for state — pair with icon/text.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`.
- Avoid flashes ≥3 Hz.

## Real-time gotchas
- Don't yank focus on incoming frames.
- Score updates: `aria-live="polite"` + `aria-atomic="true"`.
- Discoverable "reduced motion" toggle for game animations.

## Testing
- `@axe-core/playwright` on every E2E spec.
- RTL queries by role + accessible name.
